Q: WASD Movement in Unity 3D A: <p>So by analyzing the code it can be stated that you are first taking the inputs of x and y. Storing it into a vector3 then you are making a new vector3 that multiply the speed with the direction to make the player move faster. First of all you should note that the first flaw in your code can be found on the line:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Vector3 direction = new Vector3(Input.GetAxis(&quot;Horizontal1&quot;), 0, Input.GetAxis(&quot;Vertical1&quot;));
</code></pre>
<p>This direction vector need to be normalized. As when the horizontal input is 1 and the vertical input is also 1 the vector3 is (1,0,1) and the magnitude of this vector is not exactly 1. This will cause the player to move diagonally in an increased speed. This problem can be solved by adding the line.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Vector3 direction = new Vector3(Input.GetAxis(&quot;Horizontal1&quot;), 0, Input.GetAxis(&quot;Vertical1&quot;)).normalized;
</code></pre>
<p>Moving on to the next flaw it can be scene that the speed is multiplied to the velocity vector even thought the velocity vector is not responsible for moving the character.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>controller.Move(direction * Time.deltaTime);
transform.position = navMeshAgent.nextPosition;
</code></pre>
<p>These two lines are only responsible for changing the character position. <code>CharacterController</code> Move function moves the player in direction of input and navMeshAgent setting the nextPosition to the current transform.position.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>velocity = transform.TransformDirection(velocity);
</code></pre>
<p>This line just gets the local space vector of the velocity vector.</p>
<p>Your movement code seems to be all over the place. You should chose one method to move the player. Which ever you feel best. The easiest to implement looking at you code is using <code>CharacterController</code></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using UnityEngine;

[RequireComponent(typeof(CharacterController))]
public class Player : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    [SerializeField] private float gravity = -9.81f;

    private CharacterController character;
    private Vector3 velocity;

    private void Awake()
    {
        character = GetComponent&lt;CharacterController&gt;();
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        float horizontal = Input.GetAxis(&quot;Horizontal&quot;);
        float vertical = Input.GetAxis(&quot;Vertical&quot;);

        Vector3 direction = new Vector3(horizontal, 0, vertical).normalized;

        if (direction != Vector3.zero)
        {
            // Move in the direction
            character.Move(direction * speed * Time.deltaTime);
        }

        // Apply gravity
        if (!character.isGrounded)
        {
            velocity.y += gravity * Time.deltaTime;
            character.Move(velocity * Time.deltaTime);
        }
        else
        {
            velocity.y = 0f; // reset gravity if grounded
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that this code only adds gravity and character movement to the player. To add rotation the following function can be added.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>private void RotateTowards(Vector3 moveDirection)
{
    if (moveDirection == Vector3.zero) return;

    Quaternion targetRotation = Quaternion.LookRotation(moveDirection);
    transform.rotation = Quaternion.Slerp(transform.rotation, targetRotation, Time.deltaTime * 10f);
}
</code></pre>
<p>and call it from update</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>if (direction != Vector3.zero)
{
    character.Move(direction * speed * Time.deltaTime);
    RotateTowards(direction);
}
</code></pre>
<p>To rotate the player Y with the camera rotation like in Third person or first person movement you will need to add these lines of code.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Get camera-relative direction
Vector3 cameraForward = cameraTransform.forward;
Vector3 cameraRight = cameraTransform.right;

cameraForward.y = 0f;
cameraRight.y = 0f;

cameraForward.Normalize();
cameraRight.Normalize();

Vector3 moveDirection = (cameraForward * vertical + cameraRight * horizontal).normalized;
</code></pre>
